<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Imamate of Futa Toro</h1><p class="st-meta">1776 CE – 1861 CE</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Fulbe Islamic state in the middle Senegal valley, born of a clerical revolution in 1776, which challenged the Atlantic slave trade in Muslims.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/futa-toro">Futa Toro</a> region along the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toroodbe">Torodbe</a> clerics rose in the 1760s and 1770s against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/denianke-kingdom">Denianke Kingdom</a> and against raids by Moors from the north. Led first by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulayman-bal">Sulayman Bal</a> and then by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-kader-kan">Abdul Kader Kan</a>, they overthrew the Denianke, and in 1776 Abdul Kader became the first almamy of a new Islamic state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The almamy was elected by a council of great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toroodbe">Torodbe</a> families, who often deposed rulers they disliked, and he had no fixed capital. The state enforced Islamic law, built mosques and schools, and forbade the sale of Muslims as slaves, demanding that French traders on the river pay tolls and respect this rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-kader-kan">Abdul Kader</a> was killed in 1807 during wars with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-bundu">Bundu</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kaarta">Kaarta</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Weakened by factional rivalry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/futa-toro">Futa Toro</a> supplied many followers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-hadj-umar-tall">Umar Tall</a>, himself a native of the region. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-faidherbe">Faidherbe</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed its western districts around 1859 and divided the rest, and the valley was absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-senegal">French Senegal</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imamate-of-futa-toro.jpg" alt="Imamate of Futa Toro" width="320" height="228"><figcaption>Image: WordforOrange · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Imamate_of_Futa_Toro,_early_19th_century.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1776 CE – 1861 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sulayman Bal, Abdul Kader Kan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pulaar</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/senegal">Senegal</a>, <a href="/countries/mauritania">Mauritania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Futa Toro, Fouta Toro</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1818&amp;focus=imamate-of-futa-toro" aria-label="Imamate of Futa Toro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4276 1615 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imamate of Futa Toro lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4705 1621h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4596 1871h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4596 1871h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Senegal. <a href="/atlas?year=1818&amp;focus=imamate-of-futa-toro">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
